<rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"><channel><language>en</language><title>Latest news from the Middle East, daily international news reports - France 24</title><description>FRANCE 24’s coverage and analyses of political and social issues across the Middle East, including Egypt, the Gulf states, Iraq, Israel, Jordan, Lebanon, Saudi Arabia, Syria, Yemen and beyond.</description><link>https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/</link><lastBuildDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 20:47:42 GMT</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/rss" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><category>The World This Week</category><title>France Protests, Israel's 'politically charged' remembrance, Ethopia, Ukraine</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-world-this-week/20261009-france-protests-israel-s-politically-charged-remembrance-ethopia-ukraine</link><description>
It’s been a week of vigils, protests and political upheaval – from Israel, where three years after the Hamas-led attacks, former hostages and mourners called for accountability over the failures of October 7th, as questions grow over the stalled path to peace in Gaza; to France, where mass demonstrations over chronic underfunding and poor conditions have drawn students, unions and hundreds of thousands of protesters into the streets, just months ahead of a presidential election. In Spain, Prime Minister Pedro Sánchez has made the housing crisis the centrepiece of a high-stakes electoral gamble, as protests intensify following the death of a pensioner whose eviction helped ignite the movement. And in the UK, questions remain over a suspected Iranian plot targeting US aircraft at RAF Fairford, after American B-1 bombers were hastily withdrawn amid warnings of a serious and immediate threat.
 

</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/84c2482c-c414-11f1-a7cb-29a2e29ae696/w:1024/p:16x9/DLS-PHOTO-MANIFS-FRANCE.png"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/84c2482c-c414-11f1-a7cb-29a2e29ae696/w:1024/p:16x9/DLS-PHOTO-MANIFS-FRANCE.png" type="image/png" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">40606eac-c40f-11f1-b42a-45f68a7aac1b</guid><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 18:28:40 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Gavin LEE</dc:creator></item><item><category>Middle East</category><title>FlyDubai co-pilot planned suicide attack on Tel Aviv airport, inspired by 9/11, prosecutor says</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261009-flydubai-co-pilot-planned-suicide-attack-on-tel-aviv-airport-inspired-by-9-11</link><description>
The Omani co-pilot who attacked the captain of FlyDubai flight 1073 had planned to crash the plane into Tel Aviv's international airport in a suicide attack, the United Arab Emirates' top prosecutor said Friday. The Israel-bound flight carrying 182 people made an emergency landing in Saudi Arabia after a descent so extreme that part of its rudder was torn away. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/277fa400-c402-11f1-a1d2-e362370cce2e/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26273581638886-1.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/277fa400-c402-11f1-a1d2-e362370cce2e/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26273581638886-1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">752941ba-c403-11f1-8cd8-9386513c9b90</guid><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 17:04:15 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/277fa400-c402-11f1-a1d2-e362370cce2e/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26273581638886-1.jpg">© Ariel Schalit, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Tête à tête</category><title>Palestinians gained 'nothing' from October 7, Egyptian businessman Naguib Sawiris says</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/t%C3%AAte-%C3%A0-t%C3%AAte/20261008-palestinians-gained-nothing-from-october-7-egyptian-businessman-naguib-sawiris-says</link><description>
In an interview with FRANCE 24, Egyptian businessman Naguib Sawiris called the October 7 attacks a "horrible act of terrorism" that brought Palestinians "only destruction", while warning of an "expansionist" Israeli government. He also said that the economic fallout from the Iran war is already "happening", defended doubling his UAE investment despite the regional conflict, and praised Syria's new president.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/7e5a9816-c254-11f1-8186-ff8cff4603f0/w:1024/p:16x9/FR-NW-PKG-EN-TETE-A-TETE-SAWIRIS-MIX-OK.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/7e5a9816-c254-11f1-8186-ff8cff4603f0/w:1024/p:16x9/FR-NW-PKG-EN-TETE-A-TETE-SAWIRIS-MIX-OK.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">1da8cd78-c257-11f1-86e8-c5214af9e234</guid><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 11:12:58 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Marc PERELMAN</dc:creator></item><item><category>Focus</category><title>Strait of Hormuz crisis fuels Cairo's plastic recycling business</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/focus/20261008-strait-of-hormuz-crisis-fuels-cairo-s-plastic-recycling-business</link><description>
In the heart of the Egyptian capital Cairo, within the labyrinthine streets of the Manshiyet Nasser district, nearly 120,000 residents process a third of the megacity's waste every day. Long marginalised, these informal recycling specialists are now undergoing a dramatic transformation due to geopolitical tensions in the Strait of Hormuz, through which polyethylene from the Gulf usually passes.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4f3e140a-c2f8-11f1-9d0f-27c2853ad5a5/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-21204913886ac75e5b296d82-03546754.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4f3e140a-c2f8-11f1-9d0f-27c2853ad5a5/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-21204913886ac75e5b296d82-03546754.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">4befadc0-c26e-11f1-afdc-05f01c7b4ba4</guid><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 09:44:31 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Mathilde DELVIGNE</dc:creator></item><item><category>Middle East</category><title>Diplomats take shelter in Saudi capital as Yemen's Houthis target Riyadh airport</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261008-diplomats-school-pupils-take-shelter-in-saudi-capital-as-yemen-s-houthis-step-up-attacks</link><description>
Foreign diplomats and international schools in the Saudi capital took safety measures on Thursday as loud blasts rattled the city and Yemen's Houthi rebels claimed missile attacks against its airport. In a further blow to Riyadh, Turkey ruled out sending troops to confront the Iran-backed militia, despite a defence pact with Saudi Arabia.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/569c98ee-c31a-11f1-9635-c55799ef8309/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26276415281654-1.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/569c98ee-c31a-11f1-9635-c55799ef8309/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26276415281654-1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">8ba344a0-c2f9-11f1-91b8-dfc25fb90ac8</guid><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 09:20:46 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/569c98ee-c31a-11f1-9635-c55799ef8309/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26276415281654-1.jpg">© Stringer, AP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>'Most Israeli citizens lost confidence' in govt: Leaders 'shift' blame, 'block' commission inquiry</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/most-israeli-citizens-lost-confidence-in-govt-pm-leaders-shift-blame-block-commission-inquiry</link><description>
Oliver Farry welcomes Guy Poran, a former Israeli Air Force officer and member of Mistaclim (Looking the Occupation in the Eye). Poran sees October 7 as a profound rupture in Israeli society following the unprecedented Hamas attack, which targeted civilians across generations. He argues that responsibility for the failures cannot be separated from the policies that preceded the attack, including the Netanyahu government's approach toward Hamas. Without political change, Poran sees little prospect of repairing relations between Israelis and Palestinians or creating the conditions for a lasting peace, which he regards as crucial to Israel's future.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a5aaf096-c2db-11f1-87fc-27c2853ad5a5/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261007-080628-081900-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a5aaf096-c2db-11f1-87fc-27c2853ad5a5/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261007-080628-081900-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">abddb3ae-c2db-11f1-8bb6-d1909eeee128</guid><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 07:42:49 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Middle East</category><title>Explosions heard in Saudi capital Riyadh after Houthis' deadly attacks on airports</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261008-saudi-arabia-strikes-deadly-houthi-attacks-airports</link><description>
Several explosions were reported on Thursday in Saudi Arabia’s capital Riyadh amid ongoing fighting between the kingdom and Yemen's Houthis. A day earlier, three people were killed and 36 wounded in attacks on Abha and Riyadh’s King Khalid international airports claimed by the Iran-backed rebel group.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/40a51f4e-b423-11f1-bcd8-ed3d9e22a40f/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-09-19T114802Z-1980126111-RC28MNAXZ2HR-RTRMADP-3-IRAN-CRISIS-SAUDI-YEMEN.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/40a51f4e-b423-11f1-bcd8-ed3d9e22a40f/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-09-19T114802Z-1980126111-RC28MNAXZ2HR-RTRMADP-3-IRAN-CRISIS-SAUDI-YEMEN.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">6e3a1a14-c2a5-11f1-8b84-f3df44e11860</guid><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 03:02:35 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/40a51f4e-b423-11f1-bcd8-ed3d9e22a40f/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-09-19T114802Z-1980126111-RC28MNAXZ2HR-RTRMADP-3-IRAN-CRISIS-SAUDI-YEMEN.jpg">© Reuters</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>The Debate</category><title>No longer the same Israel? October 7 and the future of the Jewish State</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-debate/20261007-no-longer-the-same-israel-october-7th-and-the-future-of-the-jewish-state</link><description>
For now, it's still Benjamin Netanyahu's Israel. In 2023, in the immediate aftermath of the country's worst day of bloodshed in its history, pundits predicted the quick demise of the right-wing prime minister. But on the third anniversary of the October 7 attacks, Israel's still not reckoned with the security failures on the day, the root causes of the Hamas-led terror attack, the brutal war of vengeance that followed and above all, Netanyahu is still prime minister with a fair shot at re-election. Even if the Likud leader is voted out on October 27, his most serious election rivals don't exactly advocate for a radical break when it comes to the Palestinians.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f146ff42-c275-11f1-932d-4b195755b8ed/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-07T051408Z-2040734097-RC23YNA0KTNJ-RTRMADP-3-ISRAEL-PALESTINIANS-NOVA.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/f146ff42-c275-11f1-932d-4b195755b8ed/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-07T051408Z-2040734097-RC23YNA0KTNJ-RTRMADP-3-ISRAEL-PALESTINIANS-NOVA.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">5aee654e-c275-11f1-82a1-d551fdd35c45</guid><pubDate>Wed, 07 Oct 2026 22:10:13 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>François PICARD</dc:creator></item><item><category>Middle East</category><title>Israel marks three years since October 7 attacks with strikes on Gaza, political debate</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261007-israel-three-years-october-7-attack-elections</link><description>
Israel marked the third anniversary of Hamas’s October 7 attack on Wednesday with gatherings in tribute and renewed strikes on Gaza as political debate intensified ahead of elections. Gaza officials said strikes on the territory had killed five people despite a year-old ceasefire agreement.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9ff484f6-c271-11f1-b4b1-27c2853ad5a5/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D2H22CT.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9ff484f6-c271-11f1-b4b1-27c2853ad5a5/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D2H22CT.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">a8026040-c295-11f1-9109-dd7e32cd65fc</guid><pubDate>Wed, 07 Oct 2026 21:50:07 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9ff484f6-c271-11f1-b4b1-27c2853ad5a5/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D2H22CT.jpg">© AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>'It's still unclear whether Netanyahu will pay for what happened during his time in office'</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/it-s-still-unclear-whether-netanyahu-will-pay-for-what-happened-during-his-time-in-office</link><description>
As Israel marks the third anniversary of the October 7 attacks, questions linger about the warnings Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu received about them. Speaking with FRANCE 24's Mark Owen, Ayala Panievsky, Lecturer at City University of London and author of The New Censorship, explains that the allegations in the run-up to the elections "are very damaging for Netanyahu but there are some factors mitigating the risk for him", including a fragmented opposition as well as the control of major media.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/be013ffa-bc7e-11f1-adb1-177f79e345bf/w:1024/p:16x9/AP25357403714237.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/be013ffa-bc7e-11f1-adb1-177f79e345bf/w:1024/p:16x9/AP25357403714237.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">1be11542-c287-11f1-8caf-a909ffd0bc14</guid><pubDate>Wed, 07 Oct 2026 19:52:06 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>'Resilience' of Israelis as they mourn the loss of loved ones and 'the sense that we can be safe'</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261007-resilience-of-israelis-as-they-the-mourn-loss-of-loved-ones-and-the-sense-that-we-can-be-safe</link><description>
Angela Diffley is pleased to welcome Dr. Danny Brom, Founding Director of METIV, the Israel Psychotrauma Center in Jerusalem. According to Dr. Brom, as Israelis and Jewish people around the world continue to mourn the largest loss of Jewish life on a single day since the Holocaust, their fundamental sense of collective security has also been shattered. What strikes him, however, is the resilience that exists alongside the grieving. How can individuals, communities and society rebuild a sense of safety without allowing fear to dictate the future? Yet, as Dr. Brom observes, “the war is still going on,” while political leaders speak of pursuing “the ultimate victory, which no one really knows what that is.”
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4e9b41c6-c271-11f1-8565-4b195755b8ed/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261007-150336-152020-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4e9b41c6-c271-11f1-8565-4b195755b8ed/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261007-150336-152020-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">54563e86-c271-11f1-909c-55ad73d50d7a</guid><pubDate>Wed, 07 Oct 2026 17:46:29 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>October 7: Former Israeli hostage speaks to France 24</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261007-october-7-former-israeli-hostage-speaks-to-france-24</link><description>
“The terrorists pulled us out through the window. They handcuffed me and pushed us into a car that they had brought from Gaza.”
 
Former Israeli hostage Shoshan Haran speaks to France 24.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/158ad354-c255-11f1-9037-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-ISRAELI-HOSTAGE-tiktok-00-00-12-07-Still004.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/158ad354-c255-11f1-9037-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-ISRAELI-HOSTAGE-tiktok-00-00-12-07-Still004.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">3ea01bca-c253-11f1-9c88-e5b444f805de</guid><pubDate>Wed, 07 Oct 2026 13:30:20 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Perspective</category><title>'Time doesn't heal': Parents of October 7 attacks victim Aner Shapira</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/perspective/20261007-time-doesn-t-heal-parents-of-aner-shapira-killed-on-october-7-2023</link><description>
The parents of a 22-year-old Israeli man who was killed when Hamas attacked the Nova music festival on October 7, 2023 have told FRANCE 24 how they can still feel him surrounding them even though he's no longer here. Shira and Moshe Shapira say that time doesn't heal, but they are learning how to live with their loss. Their son Aner managed to throw seven grenades back towards Hamas fighters, before an eighth exploded in his hands, killing him. They spoke to us in Perspective.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/357ef1f8-c24c-11f1-ac38-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-14306598286ac63d9f40c3f4-70504155.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/357ef1f8-c24c-11f1-ac38-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-14306598286ac63d9f40c3f4-70504155.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">f877ebd4-c23c-11f1-ade5-cdcea157df53</guid><pubDate>Wed, 07 Oct 2026 12:59:46 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Stuart NORVAL</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>October 7: Israelis mark three years since Hamas attacks</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261007-october-7-israelis-mark-three-years-since-hamas-attacks</link><description>
Israel is marking the third anniversary of the October 7, 2023, Hamas terrorist attack on Wednesday with memorials across the country.
 
At the site of the Nova music festival, where more than 300 people were killed, families gathered at dawn for a moment of silence and paid tribute in front of portraits both of festival-goers and security forces who died on October 7.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/bdca8f88-c236-11f1-852d-b942c38e4bd7/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26280153593942.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/bdca8f88-c236-11f1-852d-b942c38e4bd7/w:1024/p:16x9/AP26280153593942.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">e43228ae-c23e-11f1-9258-a39aff491d10</guid><pubDate>Wed, 07 Oct 2026 11:04:39 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Press Review</category><title>Israeli papers commemorate October 7 attacks, three years on</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/press-review/20261007-israeli-papers-commemorate-october-7-attacks-three-years-on</link><description>
PRESS REVIEW – Wednesday, October 7: Papers mark the third anniversary of the October 7 attacks. But first: the high school protests that have swept across France make the front pages at home and abroad. Also, the Argentinian press waves off Lionel Messi after his last international match. Finally, a British adventurer completes a 28-year walk around the world.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/41db37e6-c23c-11f1-91d3-73f176a2394c/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-1854932026ac622dbf09dd2-99509392.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/41db37e6-c23c-11f1-91d3-73f176a2394c/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-1854932026ac622dbf09dd2-99509392.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">581f652c-c223-11f1-aa99-95927788547b</guid><pubDate>Wed, 07 Oct 2026 10:55:26 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Emily BOYLE</dc:creator></item><item><category>Middle East</category><title>How Netanyahu survived the political fallout from the October 7 attacks</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261007-how-netanyahu-survived-the-political-fallout-from-the-october-7-attacks</link><description>
Three years after the deadly Hamas-led October 7 attacks in southern Israel, Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu is fighting for political survival ahead of legislative elections later this month. Reports that Arab leaders warned him of the assault in advance have undercut his claims that Israel's security chiefs are to blame, but his political base appears to be holding.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/038a35d2-c192-11f1-a3fb-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-05T112307Z-585819929-RC2WWNA6B4RK-RTRMADP-3-ISRAEL-PALESTINIANS-GAZA-OCT-7-STATE-MEMORIAL.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/038a35d2-c192-11f1-a3fb-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-05T112307Z-585819929-RC2WWNA6B4RK-RTRMADP-3-ISRAEL-PALESTINIANS-GAZA-OCT-7-STATE-MEMORIAL.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">85c9b0a0-c191-11f1-8fef-a97902ef5ea9</guid><pubDate>Wed, 07 Oct 2026 05:24:03 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/038a35d2-c192-11f1-a3fb-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-05T112307Z-585819929-RC2WWNA6B4RK-RTRMADP-3-ISRAEL-PALESTINIANS-GAZA-OCT-7-STATE-MEMORIAL.jpg">© Debbie Hill, Reuters</source><dc:creator>Lara BULLENS</dc:creator></item><item><category>Middle East</category><title>French FM denounces 'poison' of anti-Semitism as Israel marks October 7 attacks anniversary</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261007-middle-east-live-houthis-target-airport-in-yemen-as-israel-marks-october-7-anniversary</link><description>
Israelis on Wednesday took part in commemorations for the victims of Hamas's October 7, 2023 attacks, which killed 1,200 people and saw 251 hostages taken. French Foreign Minister Jean-Noël Barrot denounced the "poison of anti-Semitism" as he attended a memorial ceremony at the site of the Nova music festival massacre. Read about the day’s events as they unfolded on our liveblog. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/8871fcc8-c26c-11f1-b6dc-27c2853ad5a5/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D2HZ8LG.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/8871fcc8-c26c-11f1-b6dc-27c2853ad5a5/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D2HZ8LG.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">538a343a-c1f9-11f1-af08-a12aa56ee2f3</guid><pubDate>Wed, 07 Oct 2026 02:46:40 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/8871fcc8-c26c-11f1-b6dc-27c2853ad5a5/w:1024/p:16x9/000-D2HZ8LG.jpg">© Ilia Yefimovich, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Middle East</category><title>Israel marks third anniversary of October 7 attack as election looms</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/middle-east/20261007-israel-marks-third-anniversary-of-october-7-attack-as-election-looms</link><description>
Israel will mark the third anniversary of the October 7, 2023 Hamas attack on Wednesday with memorial events across the country, as heightened security surrounds the commemorations and voters prepare for an election in which the deadliest day in Israel’s history is expected to remain a central issue.
